<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<xmile version="1.0">
  <header>
    <name>cloud-itonami SaaS funnel — external tenants to paying tenants</name>
    <vendor>com-junkawasaki/root, 90-docs/business</vendor>
    <caption>Every initial value and every rate below is a MEASURED, DATED figure
      from this workspace's own metrics, or an explicitly named derivation from
      two dated observations. No parameter is estimated, and revenue is
      deliberately absent because no price is measured here.

      Sources, both real files in this repository:
        A. 90-docs/business/metrics/cloud-itonami.edn, :asOf
           "2026-07-30T11:11:56.942Z" — the emitter's live read of
           itonami.cloud's own /api/fleet/metrics and Cloudflare zone.
        B. orgs/kotoba-lang/loop-system-dynamics/resources/entities-seed.edn,
           entity :cloud-itonami-saas-product, observation dated 2026-07-20,
           itself citing file A as of that date.

      STRUCTURE: signups flow into Non_Paying_Tenants; conversion moves a tenant
      from there into Paying_Tenants. Conversion is an outflow of one stock and
      an inflow of the other, so paying tenants can never exceed everyone who
      ever signed up.

      WHAT THIS MODEL CLAIMS: given 5 external tenants and 0 paying tenants
      observed on 2026-07-30, and a signup rate of 0.1 tenants/day measured
      from the 4 -> 5 change between the two dated observations, the number of
      paying tenants is bounded above by the trajectory below. The conversion
      rate used is the 95% UPPER BOUND consistent with zero conversions in 5
      trials (dynamics.core/upper-bound-rate-from-zero-events, n=5), not a
      point estimate — a point estimate would require at least one observed
      conversion, and there is none.

      WHAT IT DOES NOT CLAIM: that any tenant will convert. The measured
      conversion is 0/5. The run below is the CEILING that is still consistent
      with that measurement, which is a different and weaker statement than a
      forecast. Read Paying_Tenants as 「これ以上にはならない」, never as
      「これだけ増える」.

      NOT MODELLED: revenue. Nothing in this workspace measures the price a
      paying tenant pays, so a revenue variable would be the one fabricated
      number in an otherwise measured model.</caption>
  </header>

  <sim_specs method="euler" time_units="days">
    <start>0</start>
    <stop>90</stop>
    <dt>1</dt>
  </sim_specs>

  <model>
    <variables>
      <stock name="Non_Paying_Tenants">
        <eqn>5</eqn>
        <inflow>Tenant_Signup</inflow>
        <outflow>Conversion</outflow>
        <units>tenants</units>
        <non_negative/>
        <doc>MEASURED 5 = 5 external tenants minus 0 paying, both measured.
          Source A, :tenants {:external-total 5 :active 5
          :selfRegisteredOwners 5}, 2026-07-30. Source B recorded 4 on
          2026-07-20, which is where Tenant_Signup's rate comes from.

          CAVEAT carried from source B: itonami.cloud publishes an
          agent-executable free-tier claim path (/isco-1212/agent.json), and
          the same product reports 2,173 agent runs against 147 weekly human
          uniques. Some of these tenants may be agents evaluating the product
          rather than businesses considering a purchase. Source B records this
          as a well-evidenced open possibility, not a finding; this model
          therefore counts tenants as measured without asserting they are
          buyers.</doc>
      </stock>

      <flow name="Tenant_Signup">
        <eqn>0.1</eqn>
        <units>tenants/day</units>
        <non_negative/>
        <doc>MEASURED 0.1/day = (5 - 4) tenants / 10 days, from the two dated
          observations: 4 on 2026-07-20 (source B) and 5 on 2026-07-30
          (source A). One tenant over ten days is a thin basis for a rate and is
          stated as such: it is the observed rate over the only window this
          workspace has two endpoints for, not a trend.</doc>
      </flow>

      <stock name="Paying_Tenants">
        <eqn>0</eqn>
        <inflow>Conversion</inflow>
        <units>tenants</units>
        <non_negative/>
        <doc>MEASURED 0. Source A, :tenants {:external-paid 0} and
          :funnel {:paid 0}; Stripe reports :activeSubscriptions 0 on the same
          read. Source B independently recorded 0 on 2026-07-20 and separately
          verified the checkout mechanism as live and complete
          (stripeConfigured=true, webhookReady=true, readyForLiveCheckout=true)
          — so this zero is a measured absence of conversions, not an absent
          payment path.</doc>
      </stock>

      <flow name="Conversion">
        <eqn>Non_Paying_Tenants * Conversion_Rate_Upper_Bound / Observation_Window_Days</eqn>
        <units>tenants/day</units>
        <non_negative/>
        <doc>A CEILING, not a forecast. The per-trial upper bound is spread over
          the observation window that produced it, which is the assumption this
          flow makes explicit rather than burying: the bound is a probability per
          trial, and turning it into a per-day rate requires naming a window.
          Observation_Window_Days is that window, and it is measured.

          It is an OUTFLOW of Non_Paying_Tenants as well as an inflow of
          Paying_Tenants, so a converted tenant moves between the two stocks
          instead of appearing in one. The first version of this model omitted
          the outflow and was run before being committed: Paying_Tenants reached
          38.3 while total tenants reached 14, i.e. more paying customers than
          customers. A model that does not conserve its own population produces
          numbers that look like a projection and are arithmetic nonsense, which
          is worse than no model.</doc>
      </flow>

      <aux name="Total_Tenants">
        <eqn>Non_Paying_Tenants + Paying_Tenants</eqn>
        <units>tenants</units>
        <doc>The conservation check, carried as a variable so it is visible in
          any renderer instead of only asserted in this file's caption. It must
          equal 5 + 0.1 * t at every step, because signup is the only inflow to
          the pair and conversion only moves a tenant between them. Verified over
          all 91 steps to within 1e-9 before this model was committed. If a later
          edit breaks it, this line is where it shows.</doc>
      </aux>

      <aux name="Conversion_Rate_Upper_Bound">
        <eqn>0.4507197283469411</eqn>
        <units>dimensionless</units>
        <doc>COMPUTED, not estimated: 1 - (1 - 0.95)^(1/5) = 0.4507197283469411,
          the exact 95% upper bound on a Bernoulli rate given zero successes in
          n=5 trials. Produced by
          dynamics.core/upper-bound-rate-from-zero-events with n=5, the tenant
          count from source A. n comes from a measurement, so this bound moves
          when the measurement does: at n=4 (source B, 2026-07-20) it was
          0.527129195498412.

          This is the number that replaces both of the wrong answers — treating
          an unconverted funnel as unquantifiable, and treating it as a rate of
          exactly 0.</doc>
      </aux>

      <aux name="Observation_Window_Days">
        <eqn>10</eqn>
        <units>days</units>
        <doc>MEASURED 10 days: 2026-07-20 (source B) to 2026-07-30 (source A).
          The window over which zero of five tenants converted.</doc>
      </aux>

      <aux name="Weekly_Human_Uniques">
        <eqn>147</eqn>
        <units>uniques/week</units>
        <doc>MEASURED 147. Source A, :zone {:uniques-7d-sum 147}, itonami.cloud
          Cloudflare zone, trailing 7 days, 2026-07-30. Source B recorded 74 on
          2026-07-20.

          Held constant on purpose, and NOT wired into Tenant_Signup. Two dated
          points give a change (+73 over 10 days) but not a measured
          traffic-to-signup conversion, and multiplying traffic by an unmeasured
          conversion rate is the single most common way this kind of analysis
          goes wrong. It is carried as an observed context variable so a reader
          can see the funnel's mouth beside its throat.</doc>
      </aux>

      <aux name="Agent_Runs_Per_Week">
        <eqn>2173</eqn>
        <units>runs/week</units>
        <doc>MEASURED 2173. Source A, :agentRuns7d, 2026-07-30. Source B
          recorded 34381 on 2026-07-20 — a 94% fall, unexplained by anything in
          these two files, and recorded here rather than smoothed away. Present
          because the agent:human ratio is the reason External_Tenants carries
          its provenance caveat.</doc>
      </aux>
    </variables>
  </model>
</xmile>
